   a point on the plan; a point past either end of the wall is refused.
 * **A wall moved takes its doors and windows; a wall removed takes them too.** What is
   moved is no surer than the move. An element with elements under it is not removed.
+* **A wall moved takes with it the ends of the walls that meet it** (call 170), unless
+  moved alone (`move --alone`): an end at one of its ends goes with that end; an end on
+  it, between its ends, goes by the part of the move square to it; an end of it that
+  butts into a wall that stays slides along that wall to where the new line meets it
+  (pushed along its own length with both ends held, it does not move and is refused);
+  doors and windows keep their place in the house, and a move that would leave one
+  outside its wall is refused, naming it. Each wall changed is no surer than the move
+  and says it was moved; the reply says which followed and by how much, what stands
+  against the wall and was not moved, and which walls marked existing were moved. A
+  footing, a run or a thing set in place is moved as it always was. No saved job changes.
 * **A room is a point**; its boundary is found from the walls round it (section 5.6).
 
